= Semantic similarity — Simple =

'''Semantic similarity''' is about how similar two meanings are.

“Dog” and “cat” are similar because both are animals with many things in common.

“Dog” and “leash” are related, but they are not similar in the same way. One is an animal and the other is an object often used with it.

Computers can estimate similarity using dictionaries, knowledge graphs, statistics, or learned vectors.

A common method compares the direction of two vectors. A high score means the model represents them as close.

But a high score does not mean that the two things are identical or that the words are perfect synonyms.

